export const financeCompletionPosts=[
make('G05','fire-vs-coast-fire-vs-barista-fire','FIRE vs Coast FIRE vs Barista FIRE: Which Target Are You Measuring?','FIRE vs Coast FIRE vs Barista FIRE',
'Compare FIRE, Coast FIRE and part-time-income targets with the same spending assumptions. Check current living costs, growth dates and real purchasing power.', 'Financial independence','fire-calculator',
'FIRE targets portfolio-funded living costs; Coast FIRE targets enough invested today to grow toward a later retirement without more retirement deposits; Barista FIRE still relies on earned income. Reaching a Coast milestone does not mean you can stop paying today’s bills.',[
['three-targets','Three labels, three different questions',table('Choose the question before comparing balances',['Approach','Portfolio task','Income still needed'],[['FIRE','Fund the spending assigned to the portfolio under a chosen withdrawal plan','Other income only if explicitly included in the plan'],['Coast FIRE','Grow existing assets to a future retirement target with no new retirement deposits','Current living expenses, taxes, healthcare and other goals'],['Barista FIRE','Combine portfolio withdrawals with continuing earned income','The assumed part-time income, after its costs and taxes']])+p('These are informal planning labels, not standardized account types. State the cash flows instead of assuming everyone uses the same definition. '+tool('fire-calculator','The FIRE calculator')+' models accumulation while saving; '+tool('coast-fire-calculator','the Coast FIRE calculator')+' tests the zero-further-deposit milestone. Neither validates a retirement withdrawal strategy or employment income.')],
['shared-example','Keep one spending target and one money basis',p('In this original fictional USD example, portfolio-funded annual retirement spending is $40,000 in today’s purchasing power, including the taxes and healthcare allocated to it. A selected 4% initial withdrawal fraction gives $40,000 / 0.04 = <strong>$1,000,000</strong>. This is an arithmetic scenario, not a safe-rate recommendation. At 3% the same spending requires $1,333,333.33; at 5% it requires $800,000.')+p('For the Coast comparison, choose a 5% effective annual real growth assumption, after fees. There are no new deposits or withdrawals during growth. A 7.625% effective nominal return, zero additional fee and 2.5% inflation produce exactly the same real assumption: 1.07625 / 1.025 − 1 = 5%. Actual returns will not follow a fixed path.')],
['coast-time','Why the Coast milestone depends on time',table('One $1 million real target at age 65; zero further deposits',['Current age','Years to target','Required invested today at assumed 5% real growth'],[['35','30','$231,377.45'],['45','20','$376,889.48'],['65','0','$1,000,000.00']])+p('At 45, $1,000,000 / 1.05^20 = $376,889.48. Under that fixed assumption it grows to the later target. Withdrawing current living expenses from this same balance would break the no-withdrawal projection. At zero real growth the Coast threshold is the full $1 million; negative real growth can make the required starting balance greater than the later target.')+p('Use '+tool('future-value-calculator','Future Value')+' to reconcile a growth projection only after matching nominal/real rates and contribution timing. Entering a real return and then applying inflation again would remove inflation twice.')],
['barista-income','A Barista scenario needs an earned-income end date',p('Suppose the same person expects $24,000 a year of spendable part-time earnings after work expenses and taxes. During that employment period, the portfolio-funded gap is $40,000 − $24,000 = $16,000. At the selected 4% fraction, $16,000 / 0.04 = <strong>$400,000</strong>. This reduced arithmetic target depends on continuing earnings and is not interchangeable with the full $1 million target.')+p('If that work ends at 60, its $24,000 does not continue automatically. A dated plan must replace it through later benefits, lower spending, a larger portfolio or other evidenced income. Do not assume a particular employer provides health insurance or that a part-time job is guaranteed. Our two retirement calculators do not model this changing earned-income bridge.')],
['choose-model','Turn the label into a cash-flow plan',p('Write down the earliest withdrawal date, the last planned contribution date and when each income stream starts and stops. Separate an accessible bridge fund from assets with withdrawal restrictions. Keep emergency cash and home equity outside invested assets unless the plan explicitly and realistically uses them. Recalculate after a material change in spending, family needs, fees or time horizon.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Comparing a nominal future million with a today-dollar target; treating gross wages as spendable income; subtracting a later pension from today’s bill; and withdrawing from a supposedly untouched Coast portfolio all change the question. A target can be met in an accumulation model while the withdrawal plan remains exposed to market losses. Read '+guide('fire-withdrawal-rate-retirement-risks','the withdrawal-rate and retirement-risk guide')+' before interpreting target attainment.')],
['sources','Method and sources',p(link(investor,'Investor.gov’s compound-interest tool')+' provides a public reference for principal, contributions, time and compounding. Our targets and age comparisons are original calculations. '+link(fees,'Investor.gov explains how fund expenses reduce returns')+'. These examples use stated net-real assumptions and do not choose investments or a suitable withdrawal rate.')]
],[['Does Coast FIRE mean I can retire today?','No. The pure Coast projection leaves retirement assets invested without withdrawals or further deposits. Current expenses still need funding.'],['Is Barista FIRE always a $400,000 target?','No. That amount follows only from this guide’s $16,000 portfolio spending gap and selected 4% fraction. Income duration and later spending require a separate dated plan.'],['Can I compare the FIRE and Future Value tools directly?','Only with matched cash-flow timing, rate conventions and money basis. The FIRE tools use real purchasing power; fixed nominal deposits are a different scenario.']], 'Three distinct timelines show full portfolio funding, untouched Coast assets and continuing part-time income'),
make('G06','fire-withdrawal-rate-retirement-risks','FIRE Withdrawal Rates: Horizon, Sequence Risk and a Spending Plan','FIRE Withdrawal Rates and Risks',
'Test withdrawal-rate assumptions against retirement length, return order, tax, healthcare and asset access. Follow an original two-year sequence-risk example.', 'Retirement risk','fire-calculator',
'A selected withdrawal rate converts spending into a portfolio target; it does not guarantee that withdrawals will last. Retirement length, return order, fees, taxes, healthcare, accessible assets and willingness to change spending all need a separate drawdown plan.',[
['rate-definition','Define the withdrawal rule before selecting a percentage',p('An initial withdrawal fraction divides the first year’s portfolio withdrawal by the starting retirement portfolio. A fixed-real rule then adjusts that dollar withdrawal for inflation. Withdrawing the same percentage of each year’s changing balance is a different policy: income falls when assets fall. Neither is described adequately by writing “4%” without a rule.')+table('Original arithmetic sensitivity; not recommended rates',['Annual portfolio-funded spending','Selected initial fraction','Implied starting target'],[['$40,000','3%','$1,333,333.33'],['$40,000','4%','$1,000,000.00'],['$40,000','5%','$800,000.00']])+p(tool('fire-calculator','The FIRE calculator')+' uses this target for accumulation. It does not run a market-return simulation, forecast ruin or model retirement withdrawals. A higher selected fraction lowers the target mathematically; it does not establish a safer plan.')],
['sequence-risk','Same returns, different order, different remaining money',p('Start with a fictional $100,000 portfolio. Withdraw $10,000 at the <strong>beginning</strong> of each of two years. Ignore fees, taxes and inflation to isolate return order. Both paths use the same −20% and +25% annual returns.')+table('Independent two-year sequence demonstration; USD',['Path','Year 1 after withdrawal and return','Year 2 after withdrawal and return'],[['Loss first','(100,000 − 10,000) × 0.80 = 72,000','(72,000 − 10,000) × 1.25 = 77,500'],['Gain first','(100,000 − 10,000) × 1.25 = 112,500','(112,500 − 10,000) × 0.80 = 82,000']])+p('Both portfolios paid the same $20,000 in withdrawals, yet the second ends $4,500 higher. Without withdrawals, 0.80 × 1.25 = 1, and both would end at $100,000. Selling assets early reduces the capital available for recovery. The $10,000 withdrawal is deliberately a teaching amount, not a recommended retirement policy.')],
['horizon','Plan for the full horizon and uncertain lifetime',p('Retiring at 40 can require funding far more years than retiring at 65. A withdrawal finding for a specified horizon, asset mix and modeled success definition cannot simply be relabelled a lifetime guarantee. Include the possibility of living longer, a survivor’s changed expenses and late-life care. A smooth average return suppresses the return-order effect shown above.')+p('The '+tool('coast-fire-calculator','Coast FIRE calculator')+' is even narrower: it projects untouched retirement assets to a chosen future age. Its zero-contribution milestone does not test the years after withdrawals begin. '+guide('fire-vs-coast-fire-vs-barista-fire','Compare FIRE, Coast and part-time-income scenarios')+' before choosing which result to use.')],
['spending','Build a spending boundary that includes the actual bills',p('Separate essential expenses from discretionary spending and dated one-off costs. If annual living expenses are $40,000 but another $6,000 of withdrawal-funded taxes and $4,000 of healthcare are omitted, the required withdrawal is $50,000. At the same selected 4% fraction, the arithmetic target rises from $1 million to $1.25 million. These costs are fictional, not tax or insurance estimates.')+p('For taxable withdrawals, after-tax spending and gross withdrawals can differ. Do not apply one generic tax rate to every account or treat all benefits as tax-free. Include fees once: if a return assumption is already net of the same investment expense, do not subtract it again.')],
['access','Separate total wealth from accessible retirement funding',p('Write a dated inventory of liquid funds, account types, expected benefits and access restrictions. Home equity does not pay a bill without a real sale or financing plan. An emergency reserve already committed to short-term needs should not also be counted as invested retirement capital. Early US retirement-plan distributions can carry additional tax unless an applicable exception is met; exceptions and account rules require individual verification, not a blanket assumption.')],
['flexibility','Specify what changes after a poor market year',p('A flexible policy might reduce a named discretionary expense, postpone a purchase or revisit withdrawals at an agreed review point. It helps only if the budget actually has room to change. Write the trigger and affordable floor instead of promising that all spending can be cut. Test loss-first paths, higher costs and delayed other income separately; do not multiply arbitrary stress tests into a supposed success percentage.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Using a withdrawal rate as an expected investment return; comparing fixed-real with percentage-of-balance policies; ignoring a longer horizon; counting restricted assets as immediately spendable; and interpreting a target age as a retirement authorization can each mislead. Preserve the base scenario and document changed assumptions.')],
['sources','References and model boundary',p(link(fees,'Investor.gov’s fund-fee bulletin')+' explains the expense drag on investment returns. '+link('https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc558','IRS Topic 558')+' describes additional tax and exceptions for early distributions from retirement plans other than IRAs; it is not a substitute for the rules for your account. '+link('https://www.morningstar.com/retirement/how-find-your-perfect-withdrawal-rate-strategy','Morningstar’s withdrawal-strategy discussion')+' is further reading on horizon and spending policies; we do not quote a current recommended rate or claim its research validates these examples. The sequence and spending calculations above are original.')]
],[['Does the FIRE calculator prove a withdrawal rate is safe?','No. It is an accumulation projection with a selected target fraction. It does not model variable market returns or retirement drawdowns.'],['Why can the same average returns lead to different outcomes?','Withdrawals change how much remains invested between returns. In the worked loss-first path, less capital participates in the recovery.'],['Should taxes and healthcare be in my spending input?','Include the portion you expect the portfolio to fund. Record other income and its dates separately, and avoid counting a cost twice.']], 'Two withdrawal timelines show a loss-first path and a gain-first path ending at different balances'),
make('G07','how-to-analyze-financial-statements','How to Analyze Financial Statements: One Business, Three Reconciliations','How to Analyze Financial Statements',
'Analyze an income statement and two balance sheets. Reconcile ratios, average balances and why $20,000 fictional profit adds only $2,000 cash.', 'Financial statements','financial-ratios-calculator',
'First reconcile the statements, then calculate measures using matching dates and periods, and finally explain the difference between profit and cash. Our fictional business earns $20,000, generates $27,000 operating cash and increases cash by $2,000 after investing and financing.',[
['scope','Start with the reporting perimeter',p('Cedar Supply is an original fictional teaching business. All figures are USD for one 365-day year, with opening and closing balance sheets for the same entity. Net credit sales are $180,000 of $200,000 total revenue; the remaining $20,000 sales are cash sales. There are no acquisitions, exchange effects, asset disposals or other undisclosed movements. Interest and income taxes paid are operating cash flows here, with no change in their payables. This is a deliberately simplified US-style presentation, not a complete set of financial statements.')+p('Use the '+link('/guides/financial-ratios/','Financial Ratios reference hub')+' for definitions, and '+tool('financial-ratios-calculator','the grouped ratios calculator')+' to reproduce this dataset. Its defaults use these input amounts. A real review also needs accounting notes, revenue policies, estimates, commitments and comparable prior-period data.')],
['income-statement','Read the annual income statement',table('Cedar Supply annual income statement; fictional USD',['Line','Amount'],statementIncomeRows)+p('Revenue less $120,000 COGS gives $80,000 gross profit. Subtracting $50,000 operating expenses gives $30,000 operating profit. Here EBIT equals operating profit because no other non-operating items exist. After $5,000 interest and $5,000 income-tax expense, net income is $20,000. Included D&A is $10,000, so EBITDA is $40,000. Rebuild that subtotal in '+tool('ebitda-calculator','the EBITDA calculator')+'; do not add debt principal or add D&A twice.')],
['balance-sheets','Make both balance sheets balance',table('Cedar Supply balance sheets at the beginning and end of the same year; USD',['Line','Opening','Closing'],statementBalanceRows)+p('Opening assets $180,000 equal $110,000 liabilities plus $70,000 equity. Closing assets $200,000 equal $120,000 liabilities plus $80,000 equity. Cash equivalents are already in cash; the separate $5,000 investment line is not another copy of that cash. Non-interest-bearing shareholder loans are liabilities, but are excluded from the narrowly defined interest-bearing debt input.')],
['ratios','Use period flows and average balances consistently',table('Selected reconciled measures; see the hub for the full reference',['Question','Calculation','Result'],[['Liquidity at closing','60,000 / 30,000','Current ratio 2.00×'],['Assets excluding inventory/prepayments','(10,000 + 5,000 + 25,000) / 30,000','Quick ratio 1.3333×'],['Inventory efficiency','120,000 / ((22,000 + 18,000) / 2)','6.00 turns in this year'],['Collection period','((20,000 + 25,000) / 2) / 180,000 × 365','45.625 days'],['Return on assets','20,000 / ((180,000 + 200,000) / 2) × 100','10.5263% for this year'],['Return on equity','20,000 / ((70,000 + 80,000) / 2) × 100','26.6667% for this year']])+p('An opening-to-closing average uses two observations, not every daily balance. Seasonal swings can make it unrepresentative; use more frequent observations when available. A 90-day flow divided by an annual balance convention is not automatically comparable with this 365-day example. Do not assume missing opening inventory is zero.')],
['leverage','Explain three different leverage answers',p('At closing, the equity multiplier is $200,000 / $80,000 = 2.50×. Total liabilities/equity is $120,000 / $80,000 = 1.50×. Interest-bearing debt/equity is ($10,000 + $50,000) / $80,000 = 0.75×. The first equals 1 plus the second because assets = liabilities + equity. It does not equal 1 plus the third because trade payables and non-interest-bearing shareholder loans are also liabilities.')+p('Inspect these methods in '+tool('financial-ratios-calculator/#leverage','the integrated leverage selector')+'. A high ROE can reflect a smaller equity base and greater financing exposure rather than better operations. Negative equity makes a conventional positive-equity comparison unsuitable; it is not a strong score.')],
['cash-bridge','Reconcile $20,000 net income to $27,000 operating cash',table('Indirect operating cash bridge under the stated policy; USD',['Item','Signed amount'],[['Net income','20,000'],['Add included noncash D&A','+10,000'],['Increase in trade receivables','−5,000'],['Decrease in inventory','+4,000'],['Increase in operating prepayments','−2,000'],['Change in trade payables','0'],['Operating cash flow','27,000']])+p('Equipment purchases are $25,000, with no disposals. Net PPE therefore rolls forward as $125,000 + $25,000 − $10,000 depreciation = $140,000. Financing receives $10,000 additional non-interest-bearing shareholder loans and pays $10,000 dividends, net zero. Thus cash rises by $27,000 − $25,000 + $10,000 − $10,000 = $2,000, from $8,000 to $10,000. Equity rolls forward as $70,000 + $20,000 net income − $10,000 dividends = $80,000.')+p('The $2,000 cash change is not the $20,000 profit or $40,000 EBITDA. Our '+guide('operating-cash-flow','operating cash flow guide')+' and '+guide('cash-flow-statement','cash flow statement guide')+' explain their separate cash classifications. Do not classify every current account as an operating adjustment or treat a new loan as sales.')],
['interpretation','Turn the reconciliation into questions, not a health score',p('Check receivables aging before concluding that $25,000 will be collected promptly. Ask whether inventory reductions reflect efficiency, shortages or a write-down; this fictional bridge assumes no write-down. Review loan terms and related-party repayment demands rather than treating non-interest-bearing financing as risk-free. Compare margins and turnover with the company’s own history and genuinely comparable businesses, including reporting policies and seasonality.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Mixing total and credit sales in DSO, confusing total liabilities with interest-bearing debt, annualizing a short period silently, treating profit as distributions available and interpreting one ratio in isolation can reverse the decision. Follow '+guide('how-to-calculate-days-sales-outstanding','the DSO method')+' or '+guide('how-to-calculate-working-capital','the existing working-capital guide')+' when those specific balances need investigation.')],
['sources','Sources and accounting boundary',p(link(sec,'The SEC’s financial-statement introduction')+' distinguishes reporting-period flows, dated balance sheets and cash flows. '+link(ratiosSource,'BDC’s financial tools')+' organize business-ratio questions. Cedar Supply, its statement amounts, the 19-measure reference and all reconciliations are original. This teaching example omits the full notes, disclosures and complexities needed for actual reporting or lending.')]
],[['Why use average assets instead of closing assets for ROA?','The income covers a period. A matched opening/closing average is a simple estimate of the assets supporting that period, although seasonal businesses may need more observations.'],['Does the $27,000 operating cash mean owners can withdraw $27,000?','No. Equipment purchases, financing, liquidity needs and other commitments remain. This example finishes with a $2,000 cash increase after its stated movements.'],['Why is interest-bearing debt smaller than total liabilities?','Total liabilities also include payables and non-interest-bearing loans here. State the debt definition before comparing ratios.']], 'An income statement and two dated balance sheets connect to a separate operating cash reconciliation'),
make('G08','find-missing-value-equivalent-ratios','Find a Missing Value in Equivalent Ratios: All Four Positions','Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios',
'Solve a missing term in any position, verify cross products and distinguish a unique answer from inconsistent, invalid and nonunique zero cases.', 'Ratio math','equivalent-ratio-calculator',
'For a:b = c:d with valid nonzero denominators, use ad = bc, isolate the missing value, then substitute it back into the original ratios. A zero coefficient needs a consistency check before any division.',[
['setup','Label positions and units before multiplying',p('Suppose a:b = c:d means a/b = c/d, with b and d nonzero. Equivalent ratios multiply every component by the same nonzero factor. Keep the same unit order on both sides: flour:water cannot be compared directly with water:flour. Use '+tool('equivalent-ratio-calculator','the exact equivalent ratio calculator')+' in solve mode with exactly one blank or x. This guide explains the reasoning; the tool performs exact rational checks.')],
['four-positions','Solve the unknown in every position',table('One relation, four placements; each worked answer is independently substituted',['Missing position','Example','Cross-product equation','Solution and check'],[['a','x:5 = 12:20','20x = 5 × 12 = 60','x = 3; 3/5 = 12/20'],['b','3:x = 12:20','3 × 20 = 12x','x = 5; 3/5 = 12/20'],['c','3:5 = x:20','3 × 20 = 5x','x = 12; 3/5 = 12/20'],['d','3:5 = 12:x','3x = 5 × 12','x = 20; 3/5 = 12/20']])+p('In general, a = bc/d, b = ad/c, c = ad/b and d = bc/a, but only when the divisor is nonzero and the original relation is valid. The algebraic product equation alone can admit an invalid denominator, so always check the original fractions after solving.')],
['worked-steps','Work through 3:5 = x:20 without a shortcut',p('First write 3/5 = x/20. Multiply both sides by 5 × 20 to get 3 × 20 = 5x. Thus 60 = 5x, so x = 12. The scale from 5 to 20 is four; the corresponding first term must also scale by four, from 3 to 12. Checking 3 × 20 = 5 × 12 = 60 verifies the answer.')+p('Adding 15 to both 3 and 5 would give 18:20, which is not the same ratio: 3 × 20 = 60 whereas 5 × 18 = 90. Equivalent ratios preserve multiplicative relationships, not equal differences.')],
['fractions','Retain exact fractions until the final display',p('For (1/2):(3/4) = x:3, the equation is (1/2) × 3 = (3/4)x. Dividing by 3/4 gives x = 2 exactly. Multiplying both original components by four also gives 2:3. For 0.1:0.3 = 1:x, exact decimal conversion gives 1:3 = 1:x, hence x = 3. Rounding a fraction such as 1/3 before checking equality can create a false disagreement.')],
['zero-cases','A zero can mean a solution, no solution or no unique solution',table('Zero cases under the fraction convention b ≠ 0 and d ≠ 0',['Relation','Constraint','Conclusion'],[['0:5 = x:20','0 × 20 = 5x','Unique x = 0; both fractions valid'],['0:5 = 0:x','0 = 0','Any nonzero x satisfies it; no unique value'],['0:5 = 3:x','0 = 15','No solution'],['3:x = 6:0','The known denominator is zero','Invalid fraction proportion'],['0:0 = 0:5','0/0 is undefined','Invalid, not an equivalent zero ratio']])+p('Colon notation may be used for component vectors even when a later component is zero. That is a different convention from dividing a by b. The calculator accepts nonzero ratio vectors and checks proportional components; if a second term is zero, do not interpret its result as a defined fraction with a zero denominator. It rejects an all-zero vector such as 0:0. State the convention your exercise uses.')],
['three-terms','Check every known constraint in three-term ratios',p('For 2:3:4 = 6:9:x, the first two components agree on a factor of three; the third requires x = 12. In 2:3:4 = 6:10:x, the known pairs already conflict because 2 × 10 ≠ 3 × 6. No choice of x repairs them. Never solve from one convenient pair and ignore contradictory known terms.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes and next practice',p('Reversing one ratio, adding rather than scaling, rounding before cross multiplication and accepting an answer that makes a denominator zero are common errors. Write unit labels and substitute your answer. Then practice '+guide('equivalent-ratio-tables-word-problems','ratio tables, recipes and stock problems')+' to connect the symbols to quantities.')],
['sources','Method reference',p(link(proportionSource,'OpenStax’s proportions lesson')+' defines equality of fractions with nonzero denominators and the cross-product method. Our four-position, fraction and zero-case examples are original. A valid algebraic answer still needs the units and domain restrictions from the question.')]
],[['Can the unknown be the second term?','Yes. Solve the cross-product equation for that term, then check that the resulting denominator is nonzero under a fraction convention.'],['Does 0 = 0 mean x is zero?','No. It means the equation supplies no unique value. Domain restrictions still apply; in 0:5 = 0:x, x must be nonzero for the fraction interpretation.'],['Why use fractions instead of rounded decimals?','Exact fractions preserve equality. Rounding intermediate values can make equivalent ratios appear different.']], 'Four labelled ratio positions connect through the two diagonal cross products'),
make('G09','equivalent-ratio-tables-word-problems','Equivalent Ratio Tables and Word Problems: Recipes and Stock','Equivalent Ratio Tables and Word Problems',
'Build equivalent ratio tables with one scale factor. Follow original 5:2 recipe and 2:1 stock examples, quantity bars and part-to-whole checks.', 'Ratio applications','equivalent-ratio-calculator',
'An equivalent-ratio table multiplies every component by the same factor. For a 5:2 mix, factors 1, 2 and 3 give 5:2, 10:4 and 15:6. A 2:1 mix belongs to a different family: 5:2 is not equivalent to 2:1.',[
['table-method','Keep one scale factor across each row',table('Flour:water by mass; original fictional recipe',['Scale factor','Flour (g)','Water (g)','Total mixture (g)'],[['1','5','2','7'],['2','10','4','14'],['3','15','6','21'],['10','50','20','70'],['20','100','40','140']])+p('Both component columns scale together, and the total scales by the same factor. The total is seven parts, so flour is 5/7 of the mixture and water is 2/7. The ratio 5:2 does not mean flour is 5/2 of the total. This is a mathematical mass example, not a tested cooking or food-safety recipe.')],
['quantity-bars','See the parts before changing the quantity',`<figure class="ratio-parts"><div><strong>Flour: 5 parts</strong><span class="ratio-blocks" aria-label="5 equal flour parts"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span></div><div><strong>Water: 2 parts</strong><span class="ratio-blocks" aria-label="2 equal water parts"><i></i><i></i></span></div><figcaption>Each block is one equal mass part. Seven blocks make the complete mixture; labels preserve the quantities without relying on color.</figcaption></figure>`+p('For 350 g total at 5:2, one part is 350 / 7 = 50 g. Flour is 5 × 50 = <strong>250 g</strong>; water is 2 × 50 = <strong>100 g</strong>. Check 250 + 100 = 350 and 250 / 100 = 5/2. If instead you are given 350 g flour, the required water is 350 × 2/5 = 140 g; the question’s starting quantity changes the calculation.')],
['stock-problem','A 2:1 stock allocation is a separate family',table('Fictional shelf plan: blue:red units, no losses',['Multiplier','Blue units','Red units','Total'],[['1','2','1','3'],['3','6','3','9'],['6','12','6','18'],['12','24','12','36']])+p('Allocate 36 units in a 2:1 ratio. There are three total parts, so each part is 12 units. Allocate 24 blue and 12 red. For 37 indivisible units, an exact 2:1 allocation of all stock is impossible: 37/3 is not an integer. You could allocate 36 and leave one unassigned, if the task permits; do not round the components independently and still claim an exact ratio.')],
['not-equivalent','Why 5:2 and 2:1 are not equivalent',p('Cross products give 5 × 1 = 5 and 2 × 2 = 4. Because those differ, no common nonzero scaling factor changes 5:2 into 2:1. The first mix has 5/7 ≈ 71.43% of its first component; the second has 2/3 ≈ 66.67%. Compare component order and units before concluding two mixes are the same.')+p('Use '+tool('equivalent-ratio-calculator','the equivalent ratio calculator')+' to check both families or generate scaled rows. For a missing entry, use '+guide('find-missing-value-equivalent-ratios','the stepwise missing-value method')+'. A table is useful for repeated scaling; cross multiplication checks an isolated unknown.')],
['unit-check','Convert units before comparing columns',p('A fictional mix contains 0.5 kg dry material and 200 g liquid, measured by mass. Convert 0.5 kg to 500 g first: 500:200 simplifies to 5:2. Calling the raw numbers 0.5:200 would compare different units and misstate the relationship. Do not convert mass to volume without an appropriate density; a gram is not automatically a milliliter.')],
['word-problem-method','Translate a word problem into a table',p('Name both quantities and choose their order. Identify whether the supplied number is one component or the whole. Divide by its corresponding base component to find the scale factor, multiply all other components, then verify the total and original relationship. If a result must be a whole stock unit, test divisibility before rounding.')+p('For example, if the 5:2 row has 60 g water, the factor is 60/2 = 30. Flour is 5 × 30 = 150 g and total mass is 210 g. If the task gives a 210 g total, divide by seven first and reach the same row. These are equivalent descriptions of that one case, not interchangeable instructions for every starting number.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Adding the same amount to both components, mixing a component-to-component ratio with a component-to-total fraction, reversing the order, ignoring units and rounding indivisible stock can all invalidate a table. Recipe proportions alone do not price ingredients or establish nutrition, shelf life or business profitability.')],
['sources','Reference and example scope',p(link(proportionSource,'OpenStax’s proportion applications')+' supplies the equality-of-ratios method and consistent quantity-order principle. All table rows, the 350 g illustration and the indivisible-stock problem above are original. They teach proportional reasoning rather than financial leverage or accounting ratios.')]
],[['Is 5:2 equivalent to 2:1?','No. Their cross products are 5 and 4. Equivalent examples include 5:2 = 10:4 and 2:1 = 6:3.'],['What if I know the total rather than one component?','Add the component parts, divide the total by that sum, then multiply each component by the resulting one-part quantity.'],['Can I round a stock allocation?','Only if the task allows an approximation, and then label the resulting ratio as approximate. Exact allocations of indivisible units require whole-number component counts.']], 'Separate 5-to-2 and 2-to-1 quantity bars keep their different total parts visible'),
make('G10','mortgage-refinance-break-even','Mortgage Refinance Break-Even: Payment Savings vs Cost at Sale','Mortgage Refinance Break-Even: Two Methods',
'Compare simple fee recovery with cash paid plus debt remaining at the same holding date. Reconcile an original fixed-rate example and an early-sale warning.', 'Refinance decisions','refinance-calculator',
'Simple payment break-even divides eligible net upfront fees by positive monthly payment savings. A fuller same-date comparison adds cash paid and remaining principal for each loan. Those measures can give different answers, especially when loan terms change.',[
['simple-method','Start with the narrow payment-savings question',p('If net upfront transaction fees are $6,000 and a stable monthly saving is $250, simple recovery is $6,000 / $250 = <strong>24 months</strong>. At month 18 only $4,500 of payment savings has accumulated, leaving $1,500 unrecovered on that cash basis. A planned sale at month 18 arrives before this 24-month threshold.')+p('This method assumes savings persist, both schedules are still in their relevant payment periods, and no fees are financed into principal. With no positive monthly saving there is no simple payment-savings break-even. That does not prove a shorter loan has no cost advantage. Use '+tool('refinance-calculator','the mortgage refinance calculator')+' to inspect both cash and balances.')],
['same-date','Compare cash and debt at one common date',p('For the current loan, cost at holding date h = payments through h + remaining principal at h. For the refinance, cost = net upfront fees + new payments through h + new remaining principal at h. Subtract refinance cost from current cost; a positive difference favors refinancing under these assumptions. The common starting debt cancels from the difference.')+p('These are undiscounted comparative amounts, not a market valuation. They include principal so that a loan cannot look cheaper merely by postponing repayment. Keep the same property and initial debt, and account separately for taxes, insurance and other housing expenses that differ. Cash-out borrowing is outside this rate-and-term example.')],
['worked-example','Reconcile one fictional five-year comparison',p('Current balance is $250,000, fixed note rate 6.5%, with 300 months remaining. The new loan is $250,000 at an assumed 5.5% fixed note rate for 300 months. Upfront transaction fees are $6,000, financed fees and lender credits are zero, and the holding period is 60 months. Other housing costs and mortgage insurance are zero in both comparison inputs. These are fictional rates, not lender offers.')+table('P&I rounded to cents; interest retains precision internally; USD',['Measure','Keep current','Refinance'],[['Monthly principal and interest','$1,688.02','$1,535.22'],['60 payments','$101,281.20','$92,113.20'],['Principal after payment 60','$226,405.26','$223,178.73'],['Net upfront fees','$0.00','$6,000.00'],['Cash plus remaining debt','$327,686.46','$321,291.93']])+p('The refinance advantage at month 60 is <strong>$6,394.53</strong> using full-precision balances before rounding. Payment savings are $152.80 per month, so the simple threshold is ceil($6,000 / $152.80) = 40 months. Payment savings of $9,168 less fees give only $3,168; the additional $3,226.53 of lower remaining debt explains the rest of the same-date advantage.')],
['sale-before-threshold','Check the date you actually expect to exit',p('A recovery date beyond the planned sale or another refinance is not recovered within your plan. Test shorter, likely and longer holding dates. A first favorable date need not remain favorable at all later dates if a long new term continues after the old loan would be paid off. Inspect the schedule rather than treating break-even as a permanent guarantee.')],
['financed-fees','Financed fees change the debt path',p('Rolling $5,000 of fees into a $250,000 refinance creates $255,000 new principal. Do not also put that same $5,000 in upfront fees. The fee and its interest are reflected in payments plus remaining debt; a simple division using zero upfront cost would hide the economic cost. Classify each charge using '+guide('refinance-closing-costs-financed-fees','the closing-cost and financed-fee ledger')+'.')],
['limits','What this comparison leaves out',p('The model assumes fixed-rate fully amortizing loans, scheduled monthly payments and no extra principal, prepayment penalties, cash-out, ARM resets or tax effects. Note rate/12 drives interest; APR is not the accrual rate. Temporary escrow/prepaid cash affects liquidity but is excluded from irreversible transaction fees here; refund timing is not modeled. Opportunity cost and discounting can change a decision and require explicit assumptions.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Using the original current-loan term instead of months remaining; comparing payments without payoff balances; counting financed charges twice; and comparing 60 months of one option with a full lifetime of another are inconsistent. For a term change, read '+guide('refinance-shorter-vs-longer-term','the shorter-versus-longer-term comparison')+' before relying on the lower-payment label.')],
['sources','Sources and independent arithmetic',p(link(cfpb,'CFPB’s Loan Estimate explainer')+' distinguishes principal-and-interest payments, other housing charges and closing disclosures. '+link('https://openstax.org/books/principles-finance/pages/8-3-loan-amortization','OpenStax explains loan amortization')+'. We independently reconciled the fictional schedules with a separate Decimal implementation using rounded regular payments and an adjusted final payoff. Actual lender timing and rounding may differ.')]
],[['What if I sell before the simple break-even month?','The assumed payment savings have not recovered the eligible upfront fees by that date. Also compare balances and total cash at your actual holding date.'],['Does no monthly saving mean refinancing cannot reduce costs?','No. A shorter term can have higher payments and lower interest or remaining debt. Evaluate matched-date cost and affordability separately.'],['Is payment break-even the same as total-cost break-even?','No. Simple recovery ignores differences in principal remaining, while a same-date cost comparison includes them.']], 'Current and new mortgage timelines meet at one shared sale date with cash and remaining debt visible'),
make('G11','refinance-closing-costs-financed-fees','Refinance Closing Costs and Financed Fees: Reconcile the Quote','Refinance Closing Costs and Financed Fees',
'Classify upfront fees, financed charges, credits, points and escrow in a fictional quote. Reconcile new principal and closing cash without double counting.', 'Refinance costs','refinance-calculator',
'Upfront fees use cash; financed fees increase the new loan balance; lender credits offset specified charges; escrow and prepaids need separate timing treatment. A low cash-to-close figure does not mean a refinance has no cost.',[
['quote-ledger','Reconcile a fictional quote before comparing rates',p('Assume a $250,000 outstanding balance with no accrued payoff interest, cash-out, cash-in or other payoff adjustments in this teaching ledger. A fictional quote has $6,000 of irreversible transaction charges. $2,000 is financed and $4,000 is allocated upfront before a $1,000 lender credit. Temporary escrow/prepaid cash is $2,500.')+table('Original simplified refinance closing ledger; USD',['Item','Treatment','Amount'],[['Origination, including any quoted points','Transaction charges','2,000'],['Appraisal','Transaction charges','600'],['Title and settlement','Transaction charges','2,400'],['Recording and other included transaction fees','Transaction charges','1,000'],['Total transaction charges','4,000 upfront + 2,000 financed','6,000'],['Lender credit','Offset upfront charges here','−1,000'],['Net cash transaction fees','4,000 − 1,000','3,000'],['Temporary escrow/prepaids','Separate liquidity assumption','2,500'],['Cash required in this simplified ledger','3,000 + 2,500','5,500'],['New loan principal','250,000 + 2,000 financed fees','252,000']])+p('Reconciliation: $6,000 charges − $1,000 credit = <strong>$5,000 net transaction fees</strong>, comprising $3,000 cash and $2,000 financed. Cash to close here is $5,500, not $5,000, because the separate $2,500 liquidity item is not the financed-fee amount. Real payoff interest and lender adjustments need their own lines; this is not a replacement Loan Estimate.')],
['calculator-entry','Enter each charge once in the calculator',p('In '+tool('refinance-calculator','the refinance calculator')+', enter upfront fees 4,000, financed fees 2,000, credits 1,000 and temporary escrow/prepaid cash 2,500. With starting balance 250,000, it displays net upfront fees 3,000, new principal 252,000 and cash needed 5,500. Enter the actual quoted note rate and term separately. The schedule includes financed principal once; never add that same 2,000 to the upfront fee field.')],
['points','Points have a price and a quoted rate trade-off',p('A discount point is 1% of the quoted loan amount. On the $252,000 new principal in a separate points example, one point is $2,520; half a point is $1,260. These are arithmetic illustrations, not additional charges to add to the $6,000 ledger above. Use the quote’s actual points amount inside the correct fee bucket.')+p('A point does not buy a universal fixed rate reduction. Compare complete offers for the same loan type and holding periods, including note rate, charges and credits. A lender credit can accompany a different rate; increasing a credit while leaving all other quote terms unchanged is not necessarily an offer a lender will make.')],
['prepaids','Escrow, prepaids and payoff timing are separate questions',p('An escrow deposit funds a reserve for bills; it is not automatically a new irreversible fee like an appraisal. Prepaid interest and prepaid tax or insurance items have different periods and economic treatment. Review what is being paid, which dates it covers, whether it replaces a payment you would otherwise make and whether any old escrow is refunded.')+p('Our calculator groups temporary closing liquidity separately and excludes it from the comparative transaction-cost subtotal. It does not forecast refunds, daily interest, tax benefits or different prepaid periods. If a supposedly temporary item is actually an incremental unrecoverable cost, this simplified exclusion will understate it: reconcile it separately or use a fuller dated cash model. Do not classify every prepaid line as recoverable cash.')],
['no-cost','Low upfront cash can shift cost into rate or principal',p('A quote described as “no closing cost” can fund charges through a larger balance or a lender-credit trade-off. It does not establish that the lifetime or same-date cost is zero. A new $252,000 principal has $2,000 more debt than the $250,000 baseline before the first payment, and interest can accrue on the extra principal.')+p('Use '+guide('mortgage-refinance-break-even','the payment and same-date break-even methods')+' for the holding-date comparison. When fees are financed, rely on the full cash-plus-balance schedule rather than zero cash fees divided by payment savings.')],
['compare-quotes','Build an offer-by-offer comparison',p('For each quote, retain its date, rate-lock assumptions, note rate, term, principal, financed charge allocation, upfront fee allocation, credits and separate closing liquidity. Compare the expected shortest, likely and longest holding dates. Check whether costs are financed, paid now, paid earlier or omitted from the simplified ledger. A different term also changes balances: see '+guide('refinance-shorter-vs-longer-term','the term-choice guide')+'.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Counting the same fee as both financed and upfront, treating a credit as unrestricted cash back, applying point percentages to the wrong principal, entering APR as note rate and labelling all cash to close as unrecoverable cost make comparisons unreliable. This calculator limits credits to the upfront fee bucket and does not model cash rebates.')],
['sources','Primary references',p(link(cfpb,'CFPB’s Loan Estimate explainer')+' helps identify the payment, closing costs and estimated cash to close. '+link(credits,'CFPB’s points and lender-credit explanation')+' describes their rate/cost trade-offs. All dollar amounts and allocations in our quote are fictional. Ask the lender to reconcile the actual disclosure, payoff and timing items before a transaction.')]
],[['Are financed closing costs free?','No. They increase the new principal and can increase interest. Include them once through the new loan schedule.'],['Should I enter all cash to close as fees?','No. Separate transaction fees from escrow, prepaids and payoff adjustments according to their actual treatment and timing.'],['Does one point always reduce the rate by a fixed amount?','No. One point describes 1% of loan amount; the rate reduction depends on the actual lender offer.']], 'A quote ledger separates cash fees, financed fees, lender credits and temporary closing liquidity'),
make('G12','refinance-shorter-vs-longer-term','Refinance to a Shorter or Longer Term: Compare Payments and Debt','Refinance: Shorter vs Longer Term',
'Compare remaining mortgage term with shorter and longer refinance examples. Check payments, lifetime interest and remaining debt at the same five-year date.', 'Mortgage term choice','refinance-calculator',
'A shorter term can raise payments while reducing interest and remaining debt. A longer term can lower payments while extending borrowing. Compare the current loan’s remaining months, each proposed term and cash plus debt at one common holding date.',[
['remaining-term','Use months remaining, not the original mortgage term',p('A current 30-year mortgage with 25 years left has 300 remaining payments, not 360. Restarting it at 360 in the baseline gives the current option time it no longer has. The following original fictional comparison uses $250,000 outstanding at 6.5% with 300 months remaining. New fixed-rate quotes use assumed 5.5% for 180, 300 or 360 months, with $6,000 upfront fees, no financed charges or credits, and a 60-month holding date. No extra principal or other housing costs are included. Rates are illustrative, not current offers.')],
['payment-interest','Compare payment pressure and remaining lifetime interest',table('Original fixed-rate schedules; regular P&I rounded to cents; USD',['Option','Term from today','Monthly P&I','Interest through its own payoff'],[['Keep current','300 months','$1,688.02','$256,404.43'],['Shorter new term','180 months','$2,042.71','$117,687.42'],['Same new term','300 months','$1,535.22','$210,565.19'],['Longer new term','360 months','$1,419.47','$261,011.49']])+p('The shorter refinance raises P&I by $354.69 per month; the longer one lowers it by $268.55. Yet the longer schedule has $4,607.06 more lifetime interest than keeping the current loan, before its $6,000 upfront fee. The shorter schedule has less lifetime interest, but the higher payment must fit the household’s actual cash commitments. Lifetime totals span different durations and are not a same-date cost comparison.')],
['holding-date','Bring every option to the same five-year date',table('After payment 60; include fees once and retain the debt still owed; USD',['Option','Payments through 60','Balance at 60','Upfront fee','Cash + balance + fee'],[['Keep current','$101,281.20','$226,405.26','$0.00','$327,686.46'],['180-month new term','$122,562.60','$188,222.40','$6,000.00','$316,785.00'],['300-month new term','$92,113.20','$223,178.73','$6,000.00','$321,291.93'],['360-month new term','$85,168.20','$231,151.68','$6,000.00','$322,319.88']])+p('Using unrounded balances, refinance advantages at month 60 are $10,901.46, $6,394.53 and $5,366.58 respectively. Rounded table components can differ from a total by a cent. The shortest loan has the highest paid cash but the lowest remaining debt. The longest has the lowest payment but owes $4,746.42 more principal than the old loan at month 60. That balance difference is why payment savings alone are incomplete.')],
['decision','Distinguish affordability from comparative borrowing cost',p('A lower modeled cost cannot make an unaffordable monthly payment feasible. Conversely, a lower payment may be useful for liquidity but is not proof of lower total interest. Record your holding date, minimum reserve and other commitments before preferring a term. The comparison is undiscounted and assumes the same starting property and debt; it does not value the investment return on payment differences or apply tax deductions.')+p('Reproduce each option by changing only new term in '+tool('refinance-calculator','the refinance calculator')+'. Review its cash and balance table, then download the complete monthly schedule. The guide’s examples use the same cents-rounded regular payment, unrounded internal interest and adjusted final payment convention.')],
['payoff-dates','Let payments stop at payoff',p('At a horizon beyond month 180, the shorter loan’s principal-and-interest payments stop after payoff. The other options may continue. At horizons beyond both payoff dates, include actual cumulative payments and zero remaining balances for both. Taxes, insurance and HOA do not disappear when the mortgage ends; enter supported housing-cost assumptions separately. The calculator does not infer early mortgage-insurance cancellation.')],
['fees-extra-payments','Keep fee financing and extra-payment ideas explicit',p('This comparison pays fees upfront. Financing them would change the new principal and schedule: use '+guide('refinance-closing-costs-financed-fees','the closing-cost ledger')+' to classify them once. Paying extra on a longer mortgage is another possible scenario, but this calculator does not model extra principal or prepayment penalties. Do not claim the scheduled 360-month row proves the result of an extra-payment strategy.')],
['mistakes','Common mistakes',p('Restarting the current loan at its original term, calling a lifetime total a five-year saving, ignoring debt remaining at sale and treating a higher payment as automatically worse are common errors. Read '+guide('mortgage-refinance-break-even','the break-even guide')+' for why a shorter loan may have no simple payment-savings threshold while still reducing matched-date cost.')],
['sources','Sources and arithmetic conventions',p(link('https://openstax.org/books/principles-finance/pages/8-3-loan-amortization','OpenStax’s amortization explanation')+' gives the fixed-payment principal-and-interest relationship. '+link(cfpb,'CFPB’s Loan Estimate explainer')+' separates loan terms and P&I from other payment components. Our term scenarios were independently reconciled with a Decimal schedule. They are educational comparisons, not recommendations, underwriting decisions or savings guarantees.')]
],[['Can a shorter refinance cost less even if the payment rises?','Yes. Faster principal repayment can reduce interest and debt at the holding date, but the higher payment must be affordable.'],['Why compare balances after five years?','If you sell or repay then, the remaining principal still has to be settled. A lower payment may have deferred rather than eliminated that debt.'],['Does a longer term always cost more?','No universal rule covers all rates, fees and holding periods. In this example it costs more over its full life but less than keeping the old loan at the five-year comparison date.']], 'Three new-term paths compare monthly payments and remaining principal at one common five-year marker')
];

export const financialRatiosHub={
 title:'Financial Ratios: Formulas, Types and Examples',description:'Use a printable reference for 19 business financial measures. Understand periods, average balances, liquidity, profitability, leverage and returns with a reconciled example.',
 image:'/images/finance-guides/financial-ratios.svg',
 faq:[['What are financial ratios?','They relate financial-statement amounts to answer a defined liquidity, profitability, leverage or efficiency question. Their usefulness depends on matched periods, definitions and context.'],['What is a good current ratio?','There is no universal threshold that proves financial health. Review collection quality, inventory liquidity, payment dates, industry and financing terms.'],['Are liabilities and interest-bearing debt the same?','No. Total liabilities also include obligations such as trade payables. Label the numerator before comparing leverage.']],
 html:section('definition','What financial ratios tell you',p('<strong>Financial ratios compare defined financial-statement amounts to answer a specific question.</strong> Liquidity asks about near-term resources and obligations; profitability relates a profit subtotal to sales; leverage describes financing; efficiency and returns connect period activity with assets or equity. A single ratio is not a business-health score. '+tool('financial-ratios-calculator','Open the Financial Ratios Calculator')+' to calculate the grouped measures with your own records.'))+
 section('categories','Choose the question before the formula',table('A question-led map',['Category','Question','Read next'],[['Liquidity','What resources support near-term obligations?',guide('how-to-calculate-working-capital','Working capital')],['Profitability','Which earnings subtotal is being compared with sales?',tool('operating-profit-margin-calculator','Operating margin')],['Leverage and coverage','What finances the assets, and what covers interest?',tool('financial-ratios-calculator/#leverage','Integrated leverage methods')],['Efficiency and returns','How do flows relate to the balances supporting them?',guide('how-to-calculate-days-sales-outstanding','Days sales outstanding')]]))+
 section('dataset','One connected fictional business',p('Cedar Supply’s original annual example uses the same inputs as the calculator defaults. It reports $200,000 net revenue, $120,000 COGS, $50,000 operating expenses including $10,000 D&A, $5,000 interest and $5,000 income-tax expense. Thus gross profit is $80,000, operating profit/EBIT $30,000, net income $20,000 and EBITDA $40,000. Net credit sales are $180,000, and the reporting period is 365 days. All amounts are fictional USD, not an industry benchmark.')+p('Closing current assets are $60,000: $10,000 cash including equivalents, $5,000 separate eligible liquid investments, $25,000 trade receivables, $18,000 inventory and $2,000 prepayments. Current liabilities are $30,000. Closing assets $200,000 equal liabilities $120,000 plus equity $80,000; defined interest-bearing debt is $60,000. Opening inventory, receivables, assets and equity are $22,000, $20,000, $180,000 and $70,000 respectively. '+guide('how-to-analyze-financial-statements','Follow the full statement and cash reconciliation')+' without duplicating that walkthrough here.'))+
 section('reference','Printable financial-ratio formula reference',p('Print this page for the in-page reference; there is no separate eBook download. “×” means times. Ratios below use closing balances for liquidity/leverage and period flows with the stated average balances for efficiency/returns. Cash already includes equivalents; do not add them again.')+table('Cedar Supply: 19 defined measures; annual period unless explicitly a dated balance',['Group','Measure','Formula','Worked result'],ratioReferenceRows))+
 section('periods','Match dates, denominators and accounting scope',p('A balance sheet is a snapshot; an income statement covers an interval. For inventory turnover, average inventory is ($22,000 + $18,000) / 2 = $20,000. For ROA it is ($180,000 + $200,000) / 2 = $190,000. More frequent observations may better capture seasonality. Do not compare a 90-day return with a 365-day return and call the difference an improvement without addressing the period.')+p('Zero sales or zero interest does not make the corresponding margin or coverage zero; the division is undefined. Missing values are unknown, not zero. Zero or negative equity makes conventional positive-equity leverage and return comparisons unsuitable. The calculator flags these boundaries rather than inventing a score.'))+
 section('leverage','Different leverage formulas legitimately give different numbers',p('Assets/equity = 2.50×, total liabilities/equity = 1.50×, and interest-bearing debt/equity = 0.75×. With positive equity and a balanced statement, assets/equity equals 1 + liabilities/equity. It does not automatically equal 1 + interest-bearing debt/equity. '+tool('financial-ratios-calculator/#leverage','Use the integrated leverage selector')+' to keep the numerators distinct. These business ratios are not trading leverage or bank regulatory-capital measures.'))+
 section('interpretation','Interpret a pattern, not a universal pass mark',p('A current ratio can rise because more overdue invoices accumulate; a gross margin can improve while collections slow; a larger equity multiplier can lift ROE without stronger operating profit. Look at customer aging, stock quality, debt maturities and operating cash alongside the ratio. Compare the same entity, accounting policy, season and period against its own history and truly comparable businesses. No universal green threshold establishes credit eligibility.')+p('Trace '+tool('ebitda-calculator','EBITDA')+' to the included expenses before interpreting its margin. '+guide('how-to-calculate-cost-of-goods-sold','COGS classification')+' affects both gross margin and inventory turnover; '+guide('operating-cash-flow','operating cash flow')+' explains why profit is not the money available to pay obligations.'))+
 section('mistakes','Common mistakes and next steps',p('Double-counting cash, mixing total sales with credit sales, using closing inventory where an average is intended, hiding negative equity and calling working capital a percentage all change the measure. Record the numerator, denominator, dates and missing data before comparing results. '+guide('how-to-analyze-financial-statements','Analyze the complete Cedar Supply statements')+' or '+tool('financial-ratios-calculator','enter a matched dataset in the calculator')+'.'))+
 section('sources','Sources and editorial scope',p(link(sec,'The SEC’s statement guide')+' explains the statement boundaries. '+link(ratiosSource,'BDC’s financial tools')+' provide a practical ratio reference. Our dataset, formula table and reconciliations are original and use the definitions printed here. Published by '+link('/about-us/','MyBreakeven')+' under our '+link('/editorial-policy/','editorial and correction standards')+'.'))
};

